sections.content:
Na krunicu se nastavljaju **Litanije Božjem milosrđu**. Počinju uobičajenim zazivima „Gospodine, smiluj se. Kriste, smiluj se“ i zazivom Presvetog Trojstva, a zatim slijedi dugi niz invokacija s istim odgovorom: **„uzdam se u tebe“**.

Zazivi zajedno daju pregled Božjeg milosrđa kroz povijest spasenja i život čovjeka. Milosrđe se u njima opisuje kao:

- **„neshvatljiva tajna Presvetoga Trojstva“** i izraz Božje najveće moći;
- milosrđe koje se objavilo u stvaranju nebeskih duhova i koje je ljude „iz ništavila pozvalo u život“;
- milosrđe koje obuhvaća svijet, daruje besmrtan život i štiti od zasluženih kazni;
- milosrđe koje se izlijeva iz Kristovih rana i izvire iz Presvetoga Srca;
- milosrđe koje je ljudima dalo Presvetu Djevicu za majku milosrđa;
- milosrđe vidljivo u ustanovljenju Crkve i u sakramentima: **neograničeno u krštenju i pokori**, **neizmjerno u sakramentu oltara i svećeništva**;
- milosrđe koje obraća grešnike, posvećuje pravednike i usavršuje svete;
- **„vrelo bolesnicima i patnicima“**, utjeha onih skršena srca i ufanje onih koji očajavaju;
- **„mir umirućih“**, milosrđe koje čuva od paklenoga ognja i olakšava patnje dušama u čistilištu;
- „neiscrpivi izvor čudesa“.

Litanije se zaključuju riječima: „Bog je milosrdan i pun samilosti, spor na srđbu i pun dobrote. Zato ću dovijeka klicati milosrđu Božjemu.“

U **završnoj molitvi** voditelj se obraća vječnom Bogu, „čije je milosrđe neistraživo i blago smilovanja neiscrpivo“. Moli da Bog umnoži milosrđe u vjernicima kako u teškim trenucima ne bi očajavali ni bili obeshrabreni, nego se s velikim pouzdanjem predali njegovoj svetoj volji, „koja je sama ljubav i smilovanje“. Molitva završava zazivom Isusa Krista kao **„kralja milosrđa“**.

sections.content_en:
The **Litany of the Divine Mercy** follows upon the chaplet. It begins with the customary petitions “Lord, have mercy. Christ, have mercy” and an invocation of the Most Holy Trinity, and then follows a long series of invocations with the same response: **“I trust in you”**.

The petitions together provide an overview of God's mercy throughout the history of salvation and the life of man. In them, mercy is described as:

- the **“unfathomable mystery of the Most Holy Trinity”** and an expression of God's greatest power;
- mercy that revealed itself in the creation of heavenly spirits and that “called people from nothingness into life”;
- mercy that embraces the world, bestows immortal life, and protects from deserved punishments;
- mercy that pours forth from Christ's wounds and springs from the Most Sacred Heart;
- mercy that gave people the Blessed Virgin as mother of mercy;
- mercy visible in the institution of the Church and in the sacraments: **limitless in baptism and penance**, **immeasurable in the sacrament of the altar and the priesthood**;
- mercy that converts sinners, sanctifies the just, and perfects the holy;
- a **“fount for the sick and suffering”**, comfort of the brokenhearted, and hope of those who despair;
- the **“peace of the dying”**, mercy that preserves from hellfire and relieves sufferings for the souls in purgatory;
- an “inexhaustible source of miracles.”

The Litany concludes with the words: “God is merciful and full of compassion, slow to anger and abounding in kindness. Therefore I will sing of the mercies of God forever.”

In the **closing prayer**, the host addresses the eternal God, “whose mercy is unfathomable and the treasury of compassion inexhaustible.” He prays that God increase mercy in the faithful so that in difficult moments they may not despair or be discouraged, but with great confidence surrender to his holy will, “which is love and mercy itself.” The prayer concludes with an invocation of Jesus Christ as the **“king of mercy”**.
